                <pb facs="0978" n="876" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg876"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.6363Am6/29: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at San José</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Chase</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-12-09T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 9,
                                1919, 5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your September 30, 2 P.M. While Department does not desire that you should make
                    request for annulment of Amory concession, you are authorized to suggest to
                    Costa Rican Government that this concession was granted against wishes of the
                    United States Government which informed the Costa Rican Government at the time
                    that it considered it most important that only approved Americans should possess
                    oil concessions in the neighborhood of Panama Canal and that Amory concession
                    did not appear to meet these requirements. At present time it is doubted whether
                    the concession is controlled by <hi rendition="#italic">bona fide</hi> American
                    interests in good standing. You may further recall to the attention of the Costa
                    Rican Government that the United States Government has declared that it would
                    not consider any claims of American citizens arising from a business transaction
                    with the Tinoco administration as worthy of its diplomatic support.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
